#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570793} :activity/id #uuid "e13664e1-acc2-5e26-9aa9-cb80edddc807" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255 :topic/id #uuid "39fa6073-0aaa-5add-b2a0-abd4ce71a06e" :topic/math-academy-id 2048} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41b6ea5c-ab1b-5a16-9523-3c6e933226f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570235} {:answer/id #uuid "6376518c-7e22-57ce-b405-b13cfe25c801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function has no horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570297} {:answer/id #uuid "76e8c735-8556-5d88-ae45-fc7b24307d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=-3$ and $y=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570338} {:answer/id #uuid "88d595c6-1012-5a5a-9e6f-6b68cfbbc410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570365} {:answer/id #uuid "91cba4ea-e346-546d-ace3-bcbdd6893c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=-3$ and $y=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570379} :answer-field/id #uuid "b11bf33c-db38-51a0-bd44-1b88637ccbd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "822263d6-7f5d-5a37-a89e-f93c0b6eb7bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39499" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad5f193a1ff21e03659789473a6e4918b6dd7ba25cd9db4e6fc0f729d0481391.png)\n\nWhat are the equations of the horizontal asymptotes of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-3.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -3\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a horizontal asymptote at $y=-3.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ also has a horizontal asymptote at $y=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "05d1b5f7-911e-5855-8309-87882fbd1201" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa107fd-e1ce-52f6-894f-3a9f5aabc4f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570140} {:answer/id #uuid "3242475e-44a7-57f1-a086-a119e64b8342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570212} {:answer/id #uuid "cf780927-5af4-57b4-8b56-612429cbc9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570486} {:answer/id #uuid "f4659fb3-0e2d-5310-bd05-4a261c0df905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570556} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1290f7-00c0-5b91-ab44-213a5c8aa6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570140} :answer-field/id #uuid "a23871ce-bc15-5531-bf06-ef1d19b4fac1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eab5bcd3-8a73-5188-a5f4-a81be3ecb0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-75999" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c12deadd41a9c2853d0d2d50ba5c19ac6ca13012d7672941c92fa5e6cc5df642.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the graph shown above?\n\nI. $y=-1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nII. $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nThis means that $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x).$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis means that $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x).$ Therefore, III is also true.\n\n- The horizontal asymptotes of $f(x)$ are $y=1$ and $y=2.$ The line $y=-1$ is not a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ because the function does not level off to $-1$ on the right nor on the left. Therefore, I is false.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "1af9142f-e256-5a70-bedb-9d90883fc27a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081f5a61-028e-52f7-9bb4-cddbe719e3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570137} {:answer/id #uuid "20cc55df-bf81-5643-9e10-2dfd34c40f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570183} {:answer/id #uuid "2935108b-6e9f-5435-b8b4-fb38e0a0a09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570192} {:answer/id #uuid "4d74dd0a-fdd6-5862-8570-0a7f632a6b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570261} {:answer/id #uuid "8a557587-3fb3-5568-a8f7-10cd9e5ed062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570192} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d9a3e9d-c479-5cb4-a591-1e8fc50740e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba3e1985-7070-5883-9f3a-6c38d6b3795b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39517" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f7dead46db0b268a9474ad842d8d6077cc84971895e005a3686ab5b18131b6c.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "4227861c-86ff-5688-a73a-852c46fa515d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168eec9d-d998-5fcf-a223-3b5d62da107e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570169} {:answer/id #uuid "32f92ea6-8864-5f96-80c4-672e19e420e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570214} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe23007-a46a-5c1e-a688-b89de9dd4d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570291} {:answer/id #uuid "73f835ab-97b9-5393-9c3f-ad23fb2c1220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570332} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1b6905-3792-5373-bed5-087a9a37fe83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570291} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3cb9a42-0764-519a-825c-1ba27120adab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "994cad67-228c-54a3-8cec-2145c453420a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47463" :question/problem "![](images/74/7471044234ec20534f33abb45d26db3c0a2876bed0fcc73844d1867b354491c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the graph shown above?\n\nI. $f(x)\\to 2$ as $x\\to -\\infty$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to 1$ as $x\\to \\infty$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\infty$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\n- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c15f7f38-970a-5da3-816b-d00e38b1d089" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570802}}] :activity/title "End Behavior of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570116}]}
